course: dictionary
lesson: 6843
title: Polite words that hide hard news
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Outplacement» is a business word, or jargon: a special word used mostly at work. Companies use it when they have to stop employing some workers, and they want the news to sound kinder.
explain: The clue is in the meaning: «"Outplacement" means help given to fired workers to find new jobs.» The word names the help, not the sacking. The harsh part, being fired, is hidden behind the soft word.
explain: This kind of word is a euphemism, a gentle word used in place of a harsh one. When you meet jargon, ask yourself: what is this really saying? Then put it into plain words, such as "the company helps workers it has sacked find new jobs."
fact: Jargon is special words used by people in one job or field, such as business.
fact: A euphemism is a polite or gentle word used instead of a harsh one.
fact: «Outplacement» means help paid for by a company for workers it has fired, to find new jobs.
fact: «Outplacement» can also mean the firing itself, described politely.
fact: Business English uses this word in many countries, so you may hear it in more than one place.
exercise: A company says it is offering «outplacement» to its staff. What is it really giving them? | A holiday for the whole team | *Help to find new jobs for workers it has fired | A new office in the city | A rise in pay for every worker
exercise: Why might a company choose the word «outplacement» instead of «fired»? | It means the workers will stay | *It sounds gentler and more polite | It is a longer word that means fired exactly | It is a word for holidays
exercise: Which word is the polite, gentle one? | Fired | Shouted | Sacked | *Outplacement
test: What is a euphemism? | *A polite word used in place of a harsh one | A word for money | A word from a foreign language | A harsh word that hurts
test: Find and copy the word in «"Outplacement" means help given to fired workers to find new jobs.» that means sacked from a job. | *fired | help | jobs | new
exam: Why might a company talk about «outplacement» when it cuts jobs? | It wants to hide its office from visitors | It is describing a holiday | It wants to give everyone a pay rise | *It wants the job cuts to sound caring, so people focus on the help
